# HNSW Vector Index — Release Audit (2026-11-21)

Scope: full fresh audit of the HNSW vector index implementation and its full-chain
integration, ahead of the first GitHub release. Baseline: working tree at commit
`64c15f6` (release binary `target/release/mydb`, newer than all sources).

Method: six independent source-review passes over non-overlapping slices
(`hnsw.rs`, `hnsw_paged.rs`, `native_hnsw.rs`+`bulk_hnsw.rs`+`quantization.rs`+`centroids.rs`,
core shared modules, storage integration, SQL/planner/executor integration), plus a
black-box matrix (recall differential vs. numpy ground truth, visibility/edge cases,
DDL lifecycle, kill -9 crash recovery, native replication, backup/restore, concurrency,
RSS stability). All reproductions were executed on dedicated local instances
(ports 4921/4931/4932/4935/4936/4947/4951/4952, data dirs `/tmp/mydb/hnswaudit-1121/data*`,
scripts in `/tmp/mydb/hnswaudit-1121/scripts/`). Historical audit reports in
`docs-internal/` were deliberately NOT consulted.

Severity rubric:
- P0: corruption / data loss / wrong results / total service loss without recovery path
- P1: severe availability or correctness defect, reproducible and recoverable only by restart
- P2: real defect in boundary/concurrency/error handling; degraded results or robustness gaps
- P3: minor defect, narrow trigger, quality or diagnosability issue
- P4: bookkeeping/comment/cosmetic

---

## 1. Verdict summary

| # | Sev | Area | Finding | Status |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| E1 | **P1** | storage integration | Interrupt (KILL / disconnect / timeout / OOM) during commit-time vector maintenance fail-stops the entire database | runtime-reproduced ×2 |
| C1 | P2 | paged backend | `BuildAdjStore` stores per-layer neighbor counts in one byte; `m0 > 255` wraps silently | code-confirmed, black-box not reproduced |
| C2 | P2 | paged backend | Navigation-cache rebuild runs under `mutation_gate.write()` and scans the whole file; repeated write→query loads freeze DML repeatedly; builder errors swallowed | code-confirmed |
| D1 | P2 | quantization | INT8 SQ8 SSD accumulates in i32; `dim ≥ 33287` silently wraps → corrupt graph topology | code-confirmed |
| F1 | P2 | executor | Filtered KNN errors out when index maintenance is pending while the identical unfiltered query silently falls back to exact scan | code-confirmed |
| B1 | P3 | hnsw.rs | `CompactHnswBuilder` recomputes SQ8 params in f32 without the normality guards of `quantization_params()` (latent: caller passes `quantizable=false` today) | code-confirmed |
| C3 | P3 | DDL | Undeclared-dimension `VECTOR` columns skip the 16 KiB page admission check for HNSW | code-confirmed |
| C4 | P3 | paged backend | Transient "out of temp disk space" degradation is detected by English error-message prefix matching | code-confirmed |
| C5 | P3 | paged backend | `repair_build_connectivity` pops one arbitrary edge from *every* saturated node instead of only bridging nodes | code-confirmed |
| C6 | P3 | paged backend | Cursor holds `mapped_navigation` read lock across a whole advance slice, stalling same-index DML | code-confirmed |
| D2 | P3 | quantization | Same-sign extreme-magnitude data overflows f32 `mid` in `CompactHnswBuilder` → all codes collapse | code-confirmed |
| E2 | P3 | storage | One failed vector index (or a maintenance-worker panic) writes the *global* `system/index-maintenance.failed` fence and blocks the whole data dir from restarting | code-confirmed |
| E3 | P3 | recovery | Recovery purge-drain loop masks real errors as `ER_INDEX_CORRUPT` | code-confirmed |
| E4 | P3 | storage | Crash during CREATE INDEX leaves orphan `.vmanifest` + empty `.vector` dirs that startup cleanup never removes | code-confirmed |
| E5 | P3 | storage | Any non-regular file entry inside `{index_id}.vector/` permanently marks the index corrupt, including for its own deletion | code-confirmed |
| F2 | P3 | planner | `mydb_hnsw_iterative_scan='relaxed_order'` suppresses the final sort: SQL `ORDER BY` output order is silently violated | code-confirmed |
| F3 | P3 | parser | `WITH (...)` must be lexically adjacent to `USING HNSW/IVF`; valid `index_option` orders are rejected with a clear error | code-confirmed |
| F4 | P3 | planner | Multi-table KNN join-driver path (`join_knn`) is unreachable dead code; comments claim a feature that does not exist | code-confirmed |
| B2 | P3 | hnsw.rs | Equal-distance closure exploration is unbounded: queries over duplicate-heavy data can traverse the whole graph | code-confirmed |
| B3 | P3 | hnsw.rs | Cursor init holds the `topology` mutex across the full greedy descent | code-confirmed |
| B4 | P3 | hnsw.rs | Heavy-delete windows: when live nodes < ef, every query degrades to a full-graph scan | code-confirmed |
| A1 | P3 | cursor/graph | All-tombstone memory graph skips the query dimension check; wrong-dimension KNN silently returns 0 rows instead of an error | code-confirmed |
| A2 | P3 | cursor | Exhaustive cursor materializes the whole node set (O(n) heap), surfacing as an OOM error instead of streaming fallback when `mydb_hnsw_max_scan_tuples=0` on large tables | code-confirmed |
| A3 | P3 | hnsw.rs | Latent lock-order inversion: cursor-init (`topology → vector lock`) vs. link Phase 4 (`vector lock → topology`); unreachable today, fragile to future refactors | code-confirmed |
| B5 | P4 | hnsw.rs | `mark_persisted` comment contract contradicts the call site (diagnostic counter only) | code-confirmed |
| C7 | P4 | paged backend | Cursor memory accounting ignores qcodes (dim bytes/node) | code-confirmed |
| D3 | P4 | quantization | PQ `pq_centers` transpose copy not charged to the derived-cache quota | code-confirmed |
| D4 | P4 | quantization | `Node.exception` flag inconsistent with payload format during PENDING_TRAINING (stats only) | code-confirmed |
| E6 | P4 | replication | Replica in-flight vector image lost from tracking when its offset==0 WAL record is pruned (leak only) | code-confirmed |
| F5 | P4 | planner/EXPLAIN | Display nits: `late materialization` printed for exact strategies; `ivf_rerank_fetch` doc comment wrong; `explained_rerank_pool` can display above budget; EXPLAIN `k=` is offset+limit | code-confirmed |

No P0 was found. The two candidate P0s that emerged during black-box testing were
refuted during verification (see §6 — both were audit-harness artifacts, and the
underlying code paths were proven crash-atomic by source review + a dedicated
8-window kill -9 scan).

---

## 2. SRC-E1 (P1): interrupt during commit-time vector maintenance fail-stops the whole database

**Category**: availability / error classification. **Confidence**: high (code path + two independent runtime reproductions + restart verification).

### Root cause

`src/storage/core/database/vector_index.rs:3842` (`vector_maintenance_error`) calls
`self.quarantine_mvcc(...)` **unconditionally**, and only *after* that does it check
`vector_maintenance_is_transient` (`:3808-3813`, which correctly whitelists
`ER_QUERY_INTERRUPTED`, `ER_QUERY_TIMEOUT`, `ER_OUTOFMEMORY`, `ER_DISK_FULL_NOWAIT`).
The transient branch only decides whether a persistent `.vector.failed` fence file is
written — but quarantine has already marked the whole MVCC state as fail-stopped
(`txn_ops.rs:941-953`), so `ensure_operational` (`txn_ops.rs:925-939`) rejects every
subsequent statement with `ER_SERVER_SHUTDOWN` until process restart.

The interrupt reaches the maintenance path because the paged HNSW insert link reuses
`search_layer`, which calls `check_cancelled()` (`src/engine/vector/hnsw_paged.rs:3834`).
The commit sequence publishes visibility (`txn_ops.rs:3365`) *before* applying pending
vector deletes / graph inserts, so a cancellation that lands in that window converts a
cleanly committed transaction into a database-wide stop.

The background maintenance path does it in the correct order
(`vector_generations.rs:642-662`, `record_vector_maintenance_job_error` classifies
transient *first*), which confirms this is an inconsistency in the commit path rather
than an intentional design.

### Reproduction

```bash
# server: fresh data dir, any port
python3 /tmp/mydb/hnswaudit-1121/scripts/kill_query_failstop.py 4932 kill_query
```

Expected output (observed):

```
exec: KILL QUERY 5
insert result: {'err': "OperationalError(1317, 'transaction 23 has a committed WAL decision but index maintenance failed; database is fail-stopped: vector index maintenance failed: table=1024 index_id=2 storage_id=1024 generation=1 stage=update: Query execution was interrupted')"}
alive: ((1,),)
FAIL-STOPPED: (1053, 'database is fail-stopped: vector index maintenance failed: ... stage=update: Query execution was interrupted')
```

A second, independent reproduction used `INSERT … SELECT` (100k rows) in an explicit
transaction with `KILL QUERY` issued during `COMMIT` (storage-slice review). In both
cases every following statement returns 1053 until restart; after restart the data was
fully consistent (WAL `VectorIndexBatch` replay completed the graph), i.e. no data
loss — this is why the severity is P1 (recoverable) rather than P0.

Client-side triggers include: `KILL QUERY`, `KILL <conn>`, driver read-timeout
disconnects, Ctrl-C on long bulk loads — any cancellation that lands inside
commit-time vector maintenance.

### Recommended fix

In `vector_maintenance_error`, classify the error *before* quarantining: transient
errors (interrupt/timeout/OOM/disk-full-nowait) must not quarantine; the WAL replay
already completes graph maintenance on restart. Additionally consider disarming the
cancel token for the post-decision maintenance phase so a KILL cannot land there.

---

## 3. Code-confirmed P2 findings

### SRC-C1 — 1-byte neighbor counter in the chunked-build adjacency sidecar

`src/engine/vector/hnsw_paged.rs:1548` (`set_neighbors` writes
`raw.len().div_ceil(4) as u8`), `:1565-1576` (`push_neighbor` saturates at 255 and
overwrites the last slot), `:1489-1491` (reader interprets the byte), `:1418-1422`
(record layout: 1 count byte per layer). DDL admits `m ∈ [2,1024]` with
`m0 = 2*m`; for `dim ≲ 128`, `m ∈ [128, 226]` passes page admission with
`m0 ∈ [256, 452]`. Writing 256 neighbors encodes count `0`; a later read returns an
empty adjacency and `materialize` (`:1580-1592`) persists the truncated topology.
The production page format uses u16 counts and is not affected.

Black-box status: **not reproduced**. A ChunkedPaged cold build with `m=128` on
20k×64 (recall 1.000 vs. exact, `MYDB_HNSW_BULK_MODE=chunked` +
`MYDB_VECTOR_STORAGE=paged`) showed no degradation; the actual call pattern may never
hand ≥256 neighbors to `set_neighbors` (the shrink may cap earlier). Fix is still
required because the encoding is objectively wrong for admitted configurations; a
unit test on `BuildAdjStore` with `m0=256` is the cheapest guard (the workspace test
binary currently does not compile, see §7).

### SRC-C2 — navigation rebuild freezes DML on large paged indexes

`src/engine/vector/hnsw_paged.rs:2716-2751` (`start_query_navigation_build` takes
`mutation_gate.write()` and scans the whole index), `:4168` (every insert invalidates
the query topology), `:5133-5135` (every search re-arms the builder). For paged
FLOAT32 indexes whose mapped navigation is unavailable (`.hvec` > `memory_target`, or
`MYDB_HNSW_MAPPED_NAVIGATION=0`), a write→query alternating load degrades into
"one full-index scan + full DML freeze per query". Builder hard errors are swallowed
at `:2738` (`if let Ok(Some(arena))`), leaving `rebuild_requested` set so the scan
repeats on every search. Fix direction: rebuild outside the write gate (double
buffering), and surface builder failures.

### SRC-D1 — INT8 SQ8 SSD i32 overflow at dim ≥ 33287

`src/engine/vector/distance.rs:848-961` accumulates `Σ(code_a − code_b)²` in i32
(AVX2/NEON/scalar). INT8 columns allow up to 65532 dimensions; at dim ≥ 33287 a
worst-case pair wraps to a negative "distance", corrupting neighbor selection during
CompactSq8 cold builds. The comment at `:850` documents the ~33k limit but nothing
enforces it. Fix: reject the SQ8 arena above the limit (fall back to exact
navigation) or switch to i64 accumulation. Not black-box reproduced (needs an INT8
column with dim > 33000 and a cold CompactSq8 build).

### SRC-F1 — filtered KNN vs. pending maintenance: inconsistent error handling

`src/engine/executor/select/plan/filtered_vector.rs:188-214` (`vector_index_open_cursor`
only swallows `ER_OUTOFMEMORY`) vs. `src/engine/executor/select/plan/scan.rs:6661-6668`
(unfiltered path catches `ER_INDEX_CORRUPT` + vector-maintenance-pending diagnostics
and falls back to exact scan). Same index state, same query shape: without `WHERE` the
query succeeds; with `WHERE` it fails. Fix: apply the same pending-maintenance fallback
in `search_filtered_ann`.

